course: euphemisms
lesson: 17
title: Soft words for hard things: school reports and teachers and animals and pets
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Schools soften bad news about trouble and failure, and families soften bad news about pets.
explain: A teacher may say "a quiet word" instead of "a telling-off", and an official report may say "special measures" for a failing school.
explain: With pets, adults often hide death from children: "Rex went to live on a farm" may mean Rex died or was put down.
explain: Vets and owners also use soft words for the operation that stops a pet from having babies: "fixed", "done", "seen to".
explain: The kind words protect feelings, but you still need to know what really happened.
fact: "Special measures" (official UK) means a failing school under close supervision.
fact: "Behaviour management" is jargon for discipline and punishment.
fact: "A quiet word" (everyday UK) means a private telling-off.
fact: "Invited to see the head" means sent to the head teacher to be punished.
fact: "Deferred success" is a joking UK phrase for failure.
fact: "Not yet" used as a grade (US) means fail.
fact: "Vivid imagination" on a report can mean the pupil tells fibs or daydreams.
fact: "Reluctant reader" means a child who dislikes or struggles with reading.
fact: "Struggling learner" means a weak student.
fact: "Late bloomer" means a child who is behind other children for now.
fact: "Well known to staff" is a joking UK way to say the pupil is often in trouble.
fact: "Put to sleep" means an animal was killed gently by a vet.
fact: "Put down" means an animal was killed, usually by a vet.
fact: "Humanely destroyed" (official) means an animal was killed by officials.
fact: "Went to live on a farm" is what adults, especially in the US, tell children when a pet has died or been put down.
fact: "Crossed the rainbow bridge" means the pet died.
fact: "Gone to doggy heaven" is a gentle, half-joking way to tell a child the dog died.
fact: "Fixed" (US) means neutered or spayed, an operation so the pet cannot have babies.
fact: "Done" (UK) means a pet has been neutered.
fact: "Seen to" (UK) means a pet has been neutered.
exercise: A news report says a school has been put in "special measures". What does it mean? | It is failing and closely watched | It won a special award | It has new safety rules | It teaches extra maths
exercise: What is "behaviour management" really about? | Discipline and punishment | Teaching good table manners | Managing the school's money | A lesson about animals
exercise: The teacher says, "I'll have a quiet word with him." What will happen? | A private telling-off | A whisper game | A secret prize | A lesson in sign language
exercise: A pupil is "invited to see the head". What does it mean? | Sent to the head teacher for punishment | Asked to a party by the head | Invited to look at a statue | Chosen as head boy
exercise: A joker calls his failed exam "deferred success". What happened? | He failed | He passed | He got top marks | He did not sit it
exercise: On a US report, a grade of "not yet" means what? | Fail | The marks are coming later | Top | The test is cancelled
exercise: What can "has a vivid imagination" mean on a report? | Tells fibs or daydreams | Paints bright pictures | Is the best at science | Has very good eyesight
exercise: What is a "reluctant reader"? | A child who dislikes or struggles with reading | A child who reads too quickly | A child who reads in secret | A child who reads aloud well
exercise: What does "struggling learner" mean? | A weak student | A pupil who wrestles | A pupil who learns fighting | A pupil with a heavy bag
exercise: What is a "late bloomer"? | A child who is behind for now | A child who is always late | A flower that opens at night | A child born in summer
exercise: A teacher says, "Oh, Danny is well known to staff." What does she mean? | Danny is often in trouble | Danny is very popular | Danny's parents are teachers | Danny helps in the office
exercise: The vet says the old cat will be "put to sleep". What will happen? | The vet will kill it gently | The cat will have a long nap | The cat will get a sleeping pill | The cat will be put to bed
exercise: "They had to put the horse down." What does it mean? | The horse was killed | The horse was lifted off a truck | The horse was made to lie down | The horse was sold
exercise: An official notice says a dangerous dog was "humanely destroyed". What happened? | The dog was killed | The dog was retrained | The dog was set free | The dog was moved to a zoo
exercise: Mum says your old dog "went to live on a farm". What probably happened? | The dog died or was put down | The dog got a job with a farmer | The dog moved to a bigger garden | The dog ran away to the hills
exercise: What does "crossed the rainbow bridge" mean about a pet? | The pet died | The pet hid | The pet jumped over a stream | The pet was lost in the rain
exercise: What does "gone to doggy heaven" mean? | The dog died | The dog went to a nice kennel | The dog went to a dog show | It was groomed
exercise: A US neighbour says her cat is "fixed". What does it mean? | It is neutered or spayed | It was repaired after an accident | It is trained to stay still | It has a new collar
exercise: A UK vet asks, "Has your dog been done?" What is she asking? | Whether the dog has been neutered | Whether the dog finished eating | Whether the dog was washed | Whether the dog is ready to go
exercise: "We had the tomcat seen to last year." What happened to the tomcat? | He was neutered | He was looked at by a doctor for a cough | He was watched by a sitter | He was punished
test: "teacher's having a quiet word with Jake, he's bricking it" — why is Jake scared? | He is getting a private telling-off | He has to whisper a speech | He won a secret prize | He lost his voice
test: A boy jokes: "I didn't fail, I achieved deferred success." What really happened? | He failed | He passed the next day | He got full marks | He never took the test
test: Grandpa: "That lad's well known to staff, I'll tell you that." What does Grandpa mean about the lad? | He is often in trouble | He works at the school | He is famous on TV | He is the best pupil
test: A little girl says her hamster "went to live on a farm". Her big sister rolls her eyes. Why? | The hamster probably died | Hamsters hate farms | The farm is too far away | The sister wanted the hamster
test: A news headline: "Primary school placed in special measures after damning report." What does it say about the school? | It is failing and will be closely supervised | It got special money for being good | It is closing for a holiday | It has new measuring tools
test: "vet said we gotta put Bella to sleep :(" — what is going to happen to Bella? | The vet will end her life gently | Bella will get a long rest at the vet | Bella will have a nap after her jab | Bella will stay the night at the vet
test: Liam reckons "reluctant reader" means someone who reads too much. Right? | No, it means a child who dislikes or struggles with reading | Yes, it means a bookworm | Yes, it means a speed reader | No, it means a child who cannot see
test: A council notice says three stray dogs were "humanely destroyed". What happened to the dogs? | They were killed | They found new owners | They were given medicine | They were set free in the woods
test: "Dad says the dog's being done on Tuesday, done with what lol" — what is happening Tuesday? | The dog is being neutered | The dog is finishing school | The dog is being cooked | The dog is being sold
test: A US report card shows "Not yet" in reading. How did the pupil do? | Failed | Passed with honours | Absent | Got the top grade
test: The teacher writes: "Amir is a late bloomer; I expect great things." What is she saying now? | Amir is behind for now but may catch up | Amir is always late to school | Amir is great at gardening | Amir is ahead of the class
test: "Oi, you, the head wants to see you. Invited, like." What does the speaker mean? | You are in trouble with the head | You are going to a party | You won a prize from the head | You are made head boy
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an animal described in an official notice as having been "humanely destroyed"? | The animal was killed | The animal was released into the wild | The animal was rehoused with a family | The animal was given medical treatment
exam: It is given that a school has been placed in "special measures". Identify the principal implication of this designation. | The school is failing and subject to close supervision | The school has been granted additional funding for excellence | The school is closed for a holiday period | The school has adopted new systems of measurement
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the statement that a domestic cat has been "fixed", as used in the United States. | The cat has been neutered or spayed | The cat has been repaired following injury | The cat has been trained to remain stationary | The cat has been fitted with an identification tag
exam: Whereas a pupil's report states that the pupil possesses "a vivid imagination", determine what may in fact be communicated. | The pupil tells untruths or daydreams | The pupil excels in visual art | The pupil has exceptional eyesight | The pupil excels in scientific enquiry
